        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90023</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Hormone receptor status may change during disease progression and influence treatment selection. We evaluated the frequency and clinical consequences of receptor discordance. <bold>Methods: </bold>Pathology reports from 327 patients with paired primary and metastatic specimens were reviewed. Discordance was defined using contemporaneous guideline thresholds. Treatment changes following metastatic biopsy were recorded. <bold>Results: </bold>Estrogen receptor discordance occurred in 12.2% of pairs and progesterone receptor discordance in 27.5%. Metastatic biopsy findings changed systemic treatment in 44 patients, most often through initiation or discontinuation of endocrine therapy. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Reassessment of receptor status in metastatic tissue affected management in a clinically meaningful subset of patients. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
        </abstract>
